Step 7: Create the Registration Form Using HTML Helpers
Open:
Views/Student/Register.cshtml
Add:
@model StudentRegistration.Models.Student
@{
 ViewBag.Title = "Student Registration";
}
<h2>Student Registration</h2>
@using (Html.BeginForm())
{
 <div>
 @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Name)
 @Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.Name)
 @Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Name)
 </div>
 <br />
 <div>
 @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Email)
 @Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.Email)
 @Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Email)
 </div>
 <br />
 <div>
 @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Password)
 @Html.PasswordFor(m => m.Password)
 @Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Password)
 </div>
 <br />
 <div>
 @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Gender)
 @Html.RadioButtonFor(m => m.Gender, "Male")
 Male
 @Html.RadioButtonFor(m => m.Gender, "Female")
 Female
 </div>
 <br />
 <div>
 @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Course)
 @Html.DropDownListFor(
 m => m.Course,
 new SelectList(new[]
 {
 "BSc Data Science",
 "BSc IT",
 "BCA",
 "MCA"
 }),
 "Select Course"
 )
 </div>
 <br />
 <div>
 @Html.CheckBoxFor(m => m.Agree)
 I agree to the terms and conditions.
 </div>
 <br />
 <input type="submit" value="Register" />
}
@if (ViewBag.Message != null)
{
 <h3>@ViewBag.Message</h3>
}
Step 8: Understand the HTML Helpers
1. Html.BeginForm()
This creates an HTML <form> element.
@using (Html.BeginForm())
{
 // Form controls
}
It is equivalent to creating a form element manually.
2. Html.LabelFor()
Creates a label for a model property.
@Html.LabelFor(m => m.Name)
It generates HTML similar to:
<label for="Name">Name</label>
3. Html.TextBoxFor()
Creates a text box.
@Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.Name)
It generates an input element associated with the Name property.
4. Html.PasswordFor()
Creates a password field.
@Html.PasswordFor(m => m.Password)
The entered characters are hidden in the browser.
5. Html.RadioButtonFor()
Creates radio buttons.
@Html.RadioButtonFor(m => m.Gender, "Male")
and:
@Html.RadioButtonFor(m => m.Gender, "Female")
The user can select one gender.
6. Html.DropDownListFor()
Creates a dropdown list.
@Html.DropDownListFor(
 m => m.Course,
 new SelectList(new[]
 {
 "BSc Data Science",
 "BSc IT",
 "BCA",
 "MCA"
 }),
 "Select Course"
)
The user can select one course.
7. Html.CheckBoxFor()
Creates a checkbox.
@Html.CheckBoxFor(m => m.Agree)
The value is stored in the Agree property.
8. Html.ValidationMessageFor()
Displays validation errors for a particular property.
@Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Name)
Because the model contains:
[Required]
public string Name { get; set; }
the Name field cannot be left empty when validation is performed.
